notes               = "Printed under the heading ... از «عاشقانه‌های ابری» ('... from Cloudy Love Poems'), which names a sequence rather than this poem — the same heading appears above gray-gaze on page 88. **Pages 102 and 103 are one poem, joined here.** Page 102 carries no date, which by the book's own habit means it runs on, but it also ends on a full stop and a complete image, so the transcription left the boundary open. The deciding evidence is my-rain on page 95, which ends های... بارانِ من... چقدر ترا دوست دارم — the identical closing formula that page 103 uses (های... آواز... چقدر تو را دوست دارم). It is a closing apostrophe, not a standalone poem, and page 103 also carries the date the poem otherwise lacks. This page is where the book's title appears **verbatim**: با بوی کاهگل و آواز پرنده, with the و. On page 72 the two phrases sit in a sentence rather than as a phrase. A bare ۱۳۶۹ takes +621. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

با تیر و کمان کودکی‌ام
در کوچه باغ‌های قدیمی
در انبوه درختان باران‌خورده
سینهٔ گنجشکی را نشانه گرفته بودم
که عاشق تو شدم.
گنجشک بر شانه‌ام نشست
و من شکارچی ماهری شدم.
از آن پس
هرگز به شکار پرنده‌ای نرفته‌ام.
هروقت دلتنگم آواز می‌خوانم
پرنده می‌آید
پرنده می‌نشیند
پرنده را می‌بویم
پرنده را می‌بوسم
پرنده را رها می‌کنم.
و چون شکار دیگری می‌شود
کودکی‌ام را می‌بینم
در کوچه باغ‌های قدیمی
در کنار دیوارهای باران‌خورده
با بوی کاهگل و آواز پرنده
به خود می‌پیچد و گریه می‌کند.
های... آواز...
چقدر
تو را
دوست دارم.
۱۳۶۹

===machine===

With the bow and arrow of my childhood
in the lanes of the old walled gardens
in the mass of rain-soaked trees
I had taken aim at a sparrow's breast
when I fell in love with you.
The sparrow settled on my shoulder
and I became a skilled hunter.
From that day on
I have never once gone hunting a bird.
Whenever I am lonely I sing
the bird comes
the bird settles
I breathe in the bird
I kiss the bird
I let the bird go.
And when it becomes someone else's prey
I see my childhood
in the lanes of the old walled gardens
beside the rain-soaked walls
with the smell of straw-and-clay and the song of a bird —
writhing, and weeping.
Hey... song...
how much
I love
you.
1369

===lantern===

With the bow and arrow of my childhood,
in the lanes of the old walled gardens,
in the mass of rain-soaked trees,
I had taken aim at a sparrow's breast
when I fell in love with you.
The sparrow settled on my shoulder
and I became a skilled hunter.
From that day on
I have never once gone hunting a bird.
Whenever I am lonely I sing —
the bird comes,
the bird settles,
I breathe the bird in,
I kiss the bird,
I let the bird go.
And when it becomes somebody else's kill
I see my childhood
in the lanes of the old walled gardens,
beside the rain-soaked walls,
with the smell of adobe and the song of a bird,
doubling over, and weeping.

Hey... song...
how much
I love
you.

1369

===translation===

===footnotes===

**The book's title is on this page, word for word:** با بوی کاهگل و آواز پرنده —
*with the smell of adobe and the song of a bird*. Elsewhere the two things are joined by an
argument (page 72 claims the one deepens the colour of the other, and the dream-Alizadeh repeats
that claim in the letter); only here are they simply set side by side, in the exact phrase that
went on the cover.

The poem is the book's clearest account of how the man became a poet rather than a hunter: he was
aiming at a sparrow's breast when he fell in love, and the sparrow landed on his shoulder instead
of dying. What he calls being *a skilled hunter* is having never hunted again — he sings, the bird
comes of its own accord, and he lets it go. The grief at the end is not for the bird but for the
child in the lanes, who is the one doubled over weeping.

